 "method": {
  "rs": "RS = derived sector index / NEPSE index per session. Sector indices are equal-weight constituent composites (data/sector-indices.json keystone, extended in-memory from the OHLC archive between the keystone's weekly appends); the NEPSE benchmark is the official index (data/index-history.json), last close on or before the sector session.",
  "rs_ratio": "RS-Ratio = 100 x RS / EMA50(RS): the relative-strength trend. Above 100 the sector's relative line sits above its own 50-session exponential trend.",
  "rs_momentum": "RS-Momentum = 100 x RS-Ratio / SMA10(RS-Ratio): above 100 the ratio itself is rising against its recent mean.",
  "quadrants": "Leading = ratio >= 100 and momentum >= 100; Weakening = ratio >= 100, momentum < 100; Lagging = both < 100; Improving = ratio < 100, momentum >= 100. The classic JdK relative-rotation construction, in the documented daily equivalent stated here.",
  "quadrant_days": "Consecutive sessions the sector has spent in its current quadrant.",
  "consistency": "Share of the last 30 quadrant sessions spent in the sector's most frequent (modal) quadrant, in percent.",
  "rotation_score": "RS-Ratio + RS-Momentum - 100: summed distance from the 100/100 centre. rotation_rank orders sectors by it; rank_change_1w is last week's rank minus today's (positive = climbed).",
  "turnover_share": "Sector turnover (archive-reported; volume x close where a bar lacks a turnover figure) as a share of total turnover across sector-mapped equities. flow_shift_pp = latest share minus the trailing 120-session mean share, in percentage points.",
  "trail": "The last 12 weekly points (every 5th session) of (RS-Ratio, RS-Momentum) \u2014 the path the sector traced across the quadrants."
 },
 "notes": [
  "Sector rows are derived equal-weight composites of constituent stocks, not official NEPSE sub-indices; sector membership is today's map applied historically (survivorship caveat).",
  "Our taxonomy has 11 sectors: Trading companies are folded into Others. The reference board's 12th row is Trading on its own.",
  "Rotation is a relative-strength map, not a buy or sell signal: a sector can lead while falling, if it falls less than the index."
 ]
}